WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.588
Hi, I'm Lentil!

00:00:02.588 --> 00:00:06.483
Google's ax promises consistent agent state and automatic recovery.

00:00:06.483 --> 00:00:08.646
Here's how both actually work.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.059
The unit of ownership is the conversation.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:15.846
The lock is a Go map inside one process, and the reference

00:00:15.846 --> 00:00:17.424
deployment runs three of them.

00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:19.915
A turn arrives.

00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:24.728
The server adds the conversation to an in-memory map, and refuses a second caller.

00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:27.887
Each step is appended to a shared event log.

00:00:27.887 --> 00:00:30.321
When the call returns, the entry is deleted.

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:31.538
No lease, no fencing.

00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:34.198
But the map lives in one process.

00:00:34.198 --> 00:00:38.357
A second replica has its own empty map, and admits the same conversation.

00:00:38.777 --> 00:00:43.125
In the author's test, both replicas were admitted, and one ran the turn twice.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.484
Replay reduces the log to two fields: the last state, and which harness ran.

00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:51.663
Nothing records which tools already ran.

00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:52.899
Post the turn.

00:00:55.236 --> 00:00:56.416
Get back tool calls.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:00:58.689
Run them.

00:00:58.689 --> 00:01:00.253
That's where the email gets sent.

00:01:00.876 --> 00:01:02.623
Only then does the cursor move.

00:01:02.623 --> 00:01:05.535
Crash in between, and a resume runs the tools again.

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:10.895
Measured: one interrupted turn plus one follow-up ran the same work three

00:01:10.895 --> 00:01:14.803
times, and took fourteen irreversible actions where six were asked for.

00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:19.522
Before, ax was a framework: you rewrote your agent as its service

00:01:19.522 --> 00:01:20.997
and spoke its message types.

00:01:21.657 --> 00:01:23.394
In September it deleted all that.

00:01:23.394 --> 00:01:26.289
Now your agent is just a command in a container.

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:30.363
Making a decision cheap is a modelling problem.

00:01:30.363 --> 00:01:33.705
Making sure it's acted on only once is a systems problem.

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:40.412
To recap: a per-process lock, a two-field replay, and effects that can repeat.

00:01:40.832 --> 00:01:42.933
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:43.533
I'm Lentil.

00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:43.833
Bye!
